/*
Problem: Binary Tree Inorder Traversal (LC 94)
Topic: Binary Tree / DFS

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Inorder traversal visits nodes in the
following order:

Left -> Root -> Right

Recursively:

1. Traverse the left subtree.

2. Visit the current node.

3. Traverse the right subtree.

Store every visited node in the answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

For every node:

• If node is NULL,
  return.

• Traverse left subtree.

• Store current node value.

• Traverse right subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

        1
         \
          2
         /
        3

Traversal:

Left of 1

↓

Visit 1

↓

Left of 2

↓

Visit 3

↓

Visit 2

Answer:

[1,3,2]

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The recursive calls ensure that every
left subtree is completely processed
before the current node.

After visiting the current node,
the right subtree is processed.

Thus the order is always:

Left -> Root -> Right

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Every node is visited exactly once.

Space : O(h)

h = height of tree

(O(log n) for balanced,
 O(n) for skewed tree)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Tree DFS (Inorder Traversal)
*/

class Solution {
public:

    void inOrderTraversal(TreeNode* root,
                          vector<int>& ans) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return;

        // Left subtree
        inOrderTraversal(root->left, ans);

        // Visit current node
        ans.push_back(root->val);

        // Right subtree
        inOrderTraversal(root->right, ans);
    }

    vector<int> inorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {

        vector<int> ans;

        inOrderTraversal(root, ans);

        return ans;
    }
};
